#!/usr/bin/env python3
# M3-160.py
r"""
MindMap -> .xmind GUI Exporter (M3-146)
========================================
Paste hierarchy text. The first entry MUST be lvl1[1] -- it becomes the root node.

New in M3-160 (Automatic Search reverted to its pre-M3-157 browser
launching: the M3-157/158/159/160 real-Chrome/login-state/block-cooldown
chain was never as reliable in practice as the plain anonymous
chrome-headless-shell launch it replaced):
  - Root cause: M3-157 introduced _wie_launch_search_browser() (real
    Chrome preference, exported login state, consent cookies, stealth
    JS, per-context locale/timezone/viewport overrides) and M3-158/159
    layered a pool-wide block-cooldown coordinator and identity-rotation
    scheme on top of it, all aimed at Google's "block/verification page"
    problem. In practice Automatic Search was simply more reliable
    before any of that existed -- every one of these fixes-for-a-fix
    added complexity and new failure surface (Chrome-channel probing,
    storage_state export/import races, cooldown bookkeeping) without
    actually being the thing that made searches succeed.
  - Fixed by reverting every Automatic Search browser launch (the
    pre-warmed pool, both single-browser paths, and both parallel worker
    pools -- IFM and GFM alike) back to the simple, known-good approach:
    a bare pw.chromium.launch() (bundled Chromium, headless, tagged for
    verified cleanup) plus browser.new_context(user_agent=WIE_USER_AGENT)
    and nothing else -- no channel switching, no exported/imported login
    state, no consent-cookie seeding, no stealth-JS injection, no
    pool-wide block-cooldown/rotation coordinator. This is exactly the
    launch path Automatic Search used prior to M3-157.
  - _wie_launch_chromium_tagged() drops the M3-157 channel/**extra
    passthrough it no longer needs. WIE_SEARCH_USE_LOGIN_STATE,
    WIE_SEARCH_CHROME_MODE, WIE_SEARCH_SHARE_GOOGLE_SESSION,
    wie_refresh_search_login_state(), and the block-cooldown/identity-
    rotation helpers introduced in M3-157/158/159 are removed along with
    it. browser_login_setup.py's saved profile is unaffected -- it still
    backs the interactive "Open Browser & Search There" mini-browser and
    the "Log In / Clear CAPTCHA" button, which never went through this
    machinery in the first place.

New in M3-161 (Automatic Search window would go quiet for a minute-plus
at a time -- log stopped updating, progress bar seemed stuck -- even
though downloads were still landing in the output folder the whole
time, and runs were slower than 154's):
  - Root cause 1 (display freezing): make_dispatcher's on_result used to
    buffer every finished item and only ever flush the NEXT un-flushed
    index in strict order, so the window "read top to bottom" even with
    several browsers racing. That meant one slow straggler (see root
    cause 2) silently held up the log/progress-bar lines for every
    already-finished LATER item too -- they'd all dump out at once the
    moment the straggler finally resolved. The run itself was never
    stuck, only its own display was.
  - Fixed by logging each result the instant it arrives instead of
    waiting for its turn -- still labeled with its own true "[N/total]",
    just no longer gated on every earlier index reporting first. The
    progress bar/status line now advance off a monotonically-increasing
    high-water mark (the largest index seen so far) instead of the
    literal index of whichever result just came in, so out-of-order
    arrivals can't make the bar visibly jump backward, and a resumed run
    still starts the bar at the right position.
  - Root cause 2 (the actual slow straggler, and part of what made runs
    feel slower overall): wie_download_and_convert / wie_download_gif
    read a candidate's bytes with a plain resp.read() inside
    urlopen(..., timeout=timeout). urllib's `timeout` only bounds each
    individual socket operation (the connect, or one single recv() call)
    -- never the request as a whole. A response that trickles data in
    slowly enough that no single recv() ever quite hits that per-op
    timeout can take minutes to finish reading even though nothing ever
    technically "timed out" -- a real run showed exactly this: one
    candidate's download sat silent for ~93 seconds (well past the 20s
    timeout setting) before finally succeeding, while every other
    parallel worker kept moving normally the whole time.
  - Fixed with _wie_read_with_deadline(): reads the response body in
    bounded chunks against a genuine wall-clock deadline, checked before
    every chunk, and raises TimeoutError once that deadline passes even
    mid-transfer -- capping the worst-case overshoot to roughly one more
    chunk's own read time instead of leaving it unbounded.

New in M3-148 (GFM/IFM Search: Google Images results needed a click
before they'd download, plus a leftover Tkinter crash-on-close race):
  - Root cause (Google results only downloadable after clicking them):
    Google Images only puts a result's real image/GIF url into its <img>
    tag once that thumbnail is clicked open -- every unclicked result's
    <img src> is just a small static JPEG proxy thumbnail
    (gstatic.com/images?...), which is never a real GIF, so
    wie_fetch_thumbnail's require_gif check correctly rejected it and the
    extractor silently skipped it as "not an animated GIF." Google
    already has each result's real url on the page before any click,
    though -- it inlines the full result data (image url included) into
    <script> tags as page-bootstrap JSON so its own UI can render without
    a click-triggered request. Added scanInlineScripts() to the injected
    toolbar JS, run once per fullScan() (page load + each "Extract"
    click, not on every scroll mutation -- too expensive to run on those),
    which regex-scans every <script> tag's raw text for embedded
    image/GIF urls (unescaping JSON's `\/`-escaped slashes first) and
    folds them into the same accumulated url set the DOM scan already
    feeds. Clicking a result to preview it still works and still adds its
    url the normal way -- this just means it's no longer required.
  - Root cause (terminal TclError crash on close, "invalid command name
    ...!button"): _ui_safe only checked self._dialog_closed BEFORE
    scheduling a worker-thread UI update via self.after(0, fn) -- not
    again once that callback actually ran. .after(0, fn) merely queues fn
    for the main loop's next pass, so a worker thread could pass the
    check with the dialog still open, queue the update, and then have the
    user close the dialog (_on_close's self.destroy()) before the main
    loop got around to running it -- the queued call then fired against
    an already-destroyed widget. Fixed by re-checking _dialog_closed
    again inside the deferred callback itself, immediately before calling
    fn(), with a try/except tk.TclError around the call as a second line
    of defense. Also routed _log() through this same _ui_safe path
    instead of touching the log widget directly -- it's called from
    background threads (the mini-browser worker, the download thread
    pool) in several places, which was the same class of unsafe
    cross-thread widget access, just not yet the one that happened to
    crash.

New in M3-147 (GFM Search GIFs: fix the injected in-page browser button
going missing after opening a new website/tab, and wrong wording):
  - Root cause (button disappearing): the mini-browser session's own code
    -- _apply_retarget's "search again" (opens a fresh site in a new tab,
    closing the old one) and _browser_session_worker's initial tab -- only
    ever relied on the context-level context.on("page", wire_page)
    listener to get the injected "Extract images" button and its
    add_init_script onto whichever page it was about to navigate.
    add_init_script only takes effect on navigations that happen AFTER
    it's called; if that event callback hadn't actually run yet by the
    time this code's own page.goto(start_url) fired -- a straightforward
    race, not something that failed the same way every time -- that
    navigation loaded with no injected button at all, which is exactly
    what reads as "the option disappeared when I opened a new tab/site."
  - Fixed with _wie_wire_page_now: wires one specific, already-in-hand
    Page object directly and synchronously, called right before goto() in
    both _browser_session_worker (the session's first tab) and
    _apply_retarget (the new tab search-again opens) -- removing the race
    entirely for the two tabs this code itself creates. The context-level
    "page" listener (_wie_ensure_context_wiring) is kept as-is for tabs it
    can't get ahead of: ones the site itself opens (window.open, a
    target="_blank" link) or the user opens by hand (Ctrl+T).
  - Also fixed: GFM's "Search GIFs" mini-browser injected the same button
    text as IFM's "Search Images" ("Extract images from this page"), even
    though GFM only ever wants GIFs. _WIE_MINI_BROWSER_TOOLBAR_JS (a fixed
    string) became _wie_mini_browser_toolbar_js(label) (a template with
    the button wording filled in per call), driven by a new
    self._wie_item_label on each dialog -- "images" on ImageSearchDialog
    (unchanged), "GIFs" on GifSearchDialog. context._wie_current_label
    carries the right label through to whichever page/tab gets wired,
    same way context._wie_current_on_report already carried the report
    handler. GFM's own leftover "Extract images" wording in its status
    messages and help text was updated to "Extract GIFs" to match.

New in M3-144 (GFM Phase 2: the state layer behind GFM's own AMX
sub-section/node-source dropdown/browser&retry/size-settings UI, which
Phase 3 builds next):
  - Phase 1 (see the "GFM (GIF for Mindmap) -- GIF search backends"
    block further down) built the three search engines and the raw
    download/verify step (wie_download_gif, _gfm_search_and_download_
    one) but had nothing yet to call them from -- no pool for their
    results to land in, no way to tell a node-for-node Automatic Search
    result apart from a manually loaded GIF, and no record of which
    "Search node names from" source a given downloaded GIF was actually
    fetched for. This phase adds exactly that bookkeeping, deliberately
    kept separate from IFM's own equivalent state (self.structure_images
    and friends) so a GIF and a static image can never satisfy each
    other's node targets by sharing a pool.
  - self.gfm_gif_pool (+ self._gfm_search_temp_dirs and
    self.gfm_gif_pool_status_var): the flat pool every downloaded/
    manually-loaded GIF lands in, mirroring self.structure_images.
  - self.gfm_auto_gif_source: mirrors self.ifm_auto_image_source --
    tags each Automatic-Search-downloaded (or Load & Resume-recovered)
    path in gfm_gif_pool with which node-source it was fetched for, so
    a future Preview/Export can filter the fallback pool down the same
    way _ifm_pool_images_for_source does and never leak one source's
    GIFs into a build made under the other.
  - self.gfm_auto_search_positional_gifs (+ its three validity-tracking
    companions, gfm_auto_search_source_path/_sheet_indices/
    _replace_existing): mirrors ifm_auto_search_positional_images and
    the fields _ifm_get_positional_images_for_text_if_valid checks a
    stored mapping against. None at index i means node i's search never
    found a usable GIF; that node is left un-GIF'd rather than
    borrowing a neighbor's, exactly like IFM's per-node mapping.
  - self.gfm_pool_allow_cycling: a small per-source policy dict (keyed
    by the same "AMX Section" / "Text Area (Condensed Data)" strings
    the node-source dropdown uses) recording, up front, which sources'
    fallback pool is allowed to cycle -- True for AMX Section, False for
    Text Area, the identical split M3-142 hard-coded at IFM's call site.
    Declaring it here as data instead of re-deriving True/False at each
    future call site means Phase 3's Preview/Export can just look it up.
  - Nothing in this phase touches the UI, Preview, or Export -- these
    are unread, unwritten fields until Phase 3 wires the AMX sub-section
    and Automatic Search flow up to them.

  NOTE (merge from M3-148 branch into this M3-146 line): GFM (GIF for
  Mindmap) above was developed on a separate branch that forked before
  the IFM Text Area multi-sheet work (M3-144 IFM/M3-145 IFM/M3-146 Main
  Export, below) existed. GFM's own "Text Area (Condensed Data)" source
  therefore still reads only self.input_text (the single active sheet),
  NOT every checked sheet the way IFM's equivalent now does -- it was
  never updated to the multi-sheet checklist this file's IFM section
  gained afterward. Everything else GFM does (AMX Section source, GIF
  search/download, Automatic Search, Preview/Export) is unaffected.

New in M3-146 (Main Export section and IFM Text Area checklist both had
the same "not every sheet, actually" gap M3-144/M3-145 already closed
for the Eq Adjuster and IFM's own search/build):
  - Main Export's own Preview/Export buttons (_preview/_export -- NOT
    the IFM section's Preview/Export, which M3-145 already fixed) still
    only ever read self.input_text, i.e. whichever ONE sheet happened
    to be active in the multi-sheet hierarchy editor, and built a
    single-sheet .xmind via build_xmind_bytes/convert_to_xmind -- every
    other sheet in the "+"/tab strip was silently dropped from the
    previewed/exported file. Both now read every sheet via
    _get_all_sheet_texts (the same helper the Eq Adjuster and IFM's Text
    Area flow already use) and, whenever more than one sheet has
    content, build through build_xmind_bytes_multi_sheet so each sheet
    lands as its own real page in the output -- falling back to the
    original single-sheet builders (which still support F-Nodes) when
    there's genuinely just one sheet. F-Nodes are skipped (never
    silently misapplied to only one of several sheets) on a multi-sheet
    build, since build_xmind_bytes_multi_sheet has no floating-topic
    support, same limitation the IFM Text Area multi-sheet build already
    lives with.
  - IFM's own "Text Area Sheets" checklist (_rebuild_ifm_text_sheet_
    checklist) had only ONE checkbox per sheet, combining "include this
    sheet in the built file" and "search/paste images into it" into a
    single box -- unlike the AMX Section's checklist just below it,
    which has always had two independent boxes (\U0001F4C4 include in output,
    \U0001F50D search for images). The Text Area checklist now carries the same
    two boxes per row, with the same behavior: unchecking \U0001F4C4 force-
    unchecks and disables that row's \U0001F50D box (nothing for a search
    result to land in on a sheet about to be left out of the build
    entirely), and a single-sheet editor keeps its \U0001F4C4 box permanently
    checked/disabled (dropping the only sheet would just build an empty
    file) while its \U0001F50D box stays interactive. _ifm_included_text_
    sheet_indices now reads the \U0001F50D set (intersected with \U0001F4C4, same
    belt-and-braces the AMX checklist's _ifm_included_sheet_indices
    already does) and a new _ifm_included_text_output_sheet_indices
    reads the \U0001F4C4 set; _ifm_build_and_paste_text_xmind builds every
    \U0001F4C4-checked sheet into the output file but only pastes images into
    the \U0001F50D-checked subset of those, and _ifm_guard / _ifm_export's
    filename-guessing were updated to check/derive from the right set
    for each purpose.

New in M3-145 (IFM Text Area: the "Text Area Sheets" checklist promised
by M3-144 actually exists and works now -- Automatic Search and
Preview/Export genuinely span every sheet, not just one):
  - _rebuild_ifm_text_sheet_checklist and _update_ifm_text_sheets_
    visibility were CALLED (from _on_sheets_changed, the dropdown's own
    trace, and panel init) but never DEFINED anywhere in M3-144 -- every
    add/close/reorder of a sheet, or switching "Search node names from"
    to "Text Area (Condensed Data)", raised an AttributeError, and the
    checklist itself never appeared. Both methods are now implemented:
    selecting "Text Area (Condensed Data)" shows a live checklist of
    every sheet in the multi-sheet hierarchy editor (self.sheets).
  - Each row in that checklist supports right-click-to-delete (reuses
    the editor tab strip's own _on_sheet_tab_right_click/_close_sheet)
    and click-and-hold-to-drag-reorder (_on_ifm_text_sheet_row_press/
    _motion/_release -> _reorder_sheet) -- both act on the SAME
    self.sheets list the tab strip above the editor manages, so
    deleting/reordering from either place keeps both views in sync.
  - Automatic Search and Preview/Export for the "Text Area (Condensed
    Data)" source previously only ever read self.input_text -- i.e.
    whichever ONE sheet happened to be active -- despite the checklist
    UI existing since M3-144. They now walk every CHECKED sheet
    (_ifm_included_text_sheet_indices / _ifm_gather_text_multi_sheet_
    targets) and build a genuine multi-sheet .xmind file
    (build_xmind_bytes_multi_sheet, one real sheet/page per checked
    editor sheet) that images get pasted into per-sheet via
    _ifm_inject_multi_sheet, exactly like a loaded multi-sheet AMX
    .xmind file already worked.
  - _ifm_get_positional_images_for_text_if_valid now validates a stored
    Automatic Search mapping against the actual checked-sheet set
    (previously hardcoded to assume sheet_indices == [0]), so a mapping
    only survives if the SAME sheets are still checked, in the same
    combined node count, exactly mirroring the AMX-file version's own
    sheet-checklist check.
  - No change to the "changes made after starting Automatic Search
    aren't picked up mid-run" behavior -- _start_ifm_auto_search already
    snapshots terms/sheet_indices once at start (see _on_sheets_changed's
    own docstring), so this fell out for free once the gather functions
    above were actually fixed to read every checked sheet.

New in M3-143 (IFM Text Area: explain WHY the positional mapping got
discarded, instead of silently falling back to the flat pool):
  - Root cause under investigation: a report that a node whose own
    Automatic Search lookup genuinely failed (shown as "Skipped" in the
    run log, never retried before the run was stopped) still ended up
    with an image in the exported/previewed file -- which M3-142's
    per-node positional mapping should make impossible, UNLESS the
    mapping itself was judged stale by _ifm_get_positional_images_for_
    text_if_valid and Preview/Export fell all the way back to
    inject_images_into_xmind_nodes' flat-pool path. That fallback pool
    places images in first-downloaded order onto target nodes in
    target-node order -- NOT node-for-node -- so a failed node that's
    simply first in target order can pick up whatever the pool's first
    entry happens to be, which is exactly what reads as "the first node
    has an image it shouldn't."
  - _ifm_get_positional_images_for_text_if_valid had four different
    reasons it could discard the mapping (no mapping yet, mapping
    belongs to an AMX Section file instead of the Text Area, the
    sheet-index/"Replace existing images" bookkeeping doesn't match, or
    the current text no longer walks to the same target-node count the
    mapping was built against) and previously gave no indication which
    one, if any, actually fired for a given Preview/Export -- it just
    returned None and the caller silently switched pools. Each branch
    now prints exactly why to the console, and a successful (mapping
    used as-is) path prints its own confirmation too, so the specific
    trigger is visible immediately instead of having to guess. The
    node-count mismatch branch is called out explicitly as the one most
    likely to explain the report above, since it's the only one of the
    four that doesn't require the user to have changed anything by hand
    (sheet indices/replace-existing/AMX-vs-Text-Area are all direct user
    actions; node count can drift on its own if something else feeding
    into the rebuilt .xmind's structure -- e.g. equations -- changed the
    topic count between the search run and this Preview/Export).
  - _ifm_build_and_paste_text_xmind also now logs, right before falling
    back, how many images the flat pool actually has available and a
    reminder that allow_cycling=False still governs it (each used at
    most once, in target order) -- so a console transcript of a
    reproduction has everything needed to confirm which path was taken
    and why, without adding any new UI.
  - This is diagnostic-only: no placement behavior changed. Once a
    repro's console output pins down which of the four reasons is
    actually firing, that specific check is the next thing to fix (or,
    if the node-count drift turns out to be the cause, whatever's
    changing the rebuilt tree's target-node count between the search
    run and Preview/Export is the next thing to fix instead).

New in M3-142 (IFM Text Area: stop the fallback image pool from
cycling/duplicating -- a run that only found images for 20 of 50 nodes
should leave 30 nodes unimaged, never repeat those same 20 images to
paper over the gap):
  - Even after M3-141's fix, whenever the Text Area flow's Automatic
    Search positional mapping goes stale (the usual case: the text was
    edited after the search ran, so the node count/order it was
    searched against no longer matches -- see
    _ifm_get_positional_images_for_text_if_valid), Preview/Export fell
    back to inject_images_into_xmind_nodes' plain flat-pool path, which
    has always cycled: `image_paths[img_cursor % len(image_paths)]`
    wraps back around to the start once every image's been used once.
    That's the right call for a pool the user deliberately loaded to
    decorate many nodes with a handful of repeating images (Load
    Images / Load Folder), but it's the wrong call for a pool built
    from Automatic Search downloads -- each of those was fetched for
    one specific node, and wrapping it around just pastes that node's
    image onto several unrelated ones, which reads as duplication.
  - Added `allow_cycling` to inject_images_into_xmind_nodes (default
    True, so every other caller -- AMX Section's own Preview/Export,
    RXF's IFM-pool path -- is completely unaffected). IFM's Text Area
    flow (_ifm_build_and_paste_text_xmind) now always passes
    allow_cycling=False: once every path in the fallback pool has been
    used exactly once, every further target node is simply left
    unimaged instead of reusing an earlier image. Combined with
    M3-141's per-source pool filtering, this means a Text Area run that
    downloaded 20/50 images -- whether its own positional mapping is
    still valid (in which case it was already node-accurate with no
    duplication, unchanged) or has since gone stale and fell back to
    the flat pool -- now places at most 20 images total, each on its
    own node, never repeated.

New in M3-141 (IFM: Automatic Search results were leaking across the
"Search node names from" dropdown -- a partially-finished run under
Text Area could end up filling EVERY node with an image, and switching
the dropdown after a run could paste the previous run's images into an
unrelated build):
  - Root cause #1 (partial Text Area run filled every node instead of
    leaving un-found ones blank): the positional mapping a run leaves
    behind is only trusted by Preview/Export if it's still tagged with
    the right "source" -- None for "Text Area (Condensed Data)", the
    loaded file's path for "AMX Section" (see _ifm_get_positional_
    images_for_text_if_valid / _ifm_get_positional_images_if_valid).
    But _run_ifm_auto_search's own `finally` block always recorded
    self.ifm_loaded_xmind_path there, regardless of which source the
    run actually searched against. The moment an .xmind file had EVER
    been loaded into IFM's AMX Section -- even if the dropdown was set
    to "Text Area" for the run itself -- that path got stamped onto a
    Text Area run's mapping too, so the very next Preview/Export saw a
    non-None source_path, decided the mapping didn't belong to the text
    box after all, and quietly discarded it. With no positional mapping
    to fall back on, it cycled through the flat structure_images pool
    instead (the same pool "Load Images"/"Load Folder" use) -- which
    only contains the images that DID download successfully -- so it
    wrapped that shorter list around to cover every node needing an
    image, including the ones Automatic Search never actually found
    anything for. Fixed by threading the run's actual node-source
    ("AMX Section" vs "Text Area (Condensed Data)", captured once up
    front in _start_ifm_auto_search/_resume_ifm_auto_search, before the
    background thread starts) through to _run_ifm_auto_search, which
    now stamps source_path as None for a Text Area run no matter what
    else happens to be loaded in the AMX Section. The mapping now stays
    valid and correctly leaves an un-found node's slot empty instead of
    borrowing whatever the pool cycles around to next.
  - Root cause #2 (switching the dropdown after a run could still paste
    that run's images into a DIFFERENT source's build): every image
    Automatic Search downloads is also appended to the same flat
    structure_images pool "Load Images"/"Load Folder"/"Search Images"
    feed (so the on-screen count and manual cycling both see it) -- but
    that pool never remembered which "Search node names from" source an
    image was actually downloaded for. So even once root cause #1's fix
    correctly invalidates a stale/mismatched positional mapping,
    Preview/Export's fallback still cycled through the WHOLE pool,
    silently including images a previous run downloaded for the other
    source. Fixed with a new self.ifm_auto_image_source dict recording,
    for each Automatic-Search-downloaded (or Load & Resume-recovered)
    path, which source it was fetched for; a new _ifm_pool_images_for_
    source() helper filters the cycling-fallback pool down to only
    images that either came from Automatic Search under the CURRENTLY
    selected source, or were never tagged at all (manually loaded/
    searched images stay source-agnostic, exactly as before). Preview,
    Export, and the "No Images Loaded" guard checks all now go through
    this filtered pool instead of the raw structure_images list.
  - Also added a trace on the "Search node names from" dropdown itself:
    switching it now immediately invalidates any stored Automatic
    Search positional mapping (the same fields a fresh search or a
    reloaded/cleared file already reset), so a stale mapping can never
    even be considered for whichever source is newly selected, on top
    of the source-tagging fix above.
  - None of this touches manually loaded/searched images (Load Images /
    Load Folder / Search Images) -- those were never source-tagged and
    keep cycling across either dropdown option exactly as before; only
    Automatic-Search-downloaded and Load & Resume-recovered images are
    now scoped to the source they actually belong to.

New in M3-129 (IFM Automatic Search: cap the pool's *combined* request
rate, not just each browser's own pacing; tell block pages apart from
genuinely-empty results):
  - Root cause: M3-127/M3-128 already added `stagger` (spread out each
    worker's first request) and `item_delay` (pause between a worker's
    own requests), but both only pace each worker against itself -- the
    pool's combined request rate to Google still scales with
    num_browsers (6 workers each waiting 0.4s between their own
    requests still fire a combined ~15 requests/sec at Google). Real
    run logs kept showing the same handful of ordinary single-word
    terms failing every single retry pass with either
    net::ERR_CONNECTION_CLOSED or a zero-candidate "no usable image
    found", which for a common word like "Banana" almost never means
    Google genuinely has nothing -- it means Google served a block/
    verification page instead of results.
  - Fixed with a shared, pool-wide floor (`min_request_interval`,
    enforced via one lock + last-request timestamp closed over by every
    worker) on the gap between ANY two requests across every browser
    combined, on top of (not instead of) the existing per-worker
    stagger/item_delay -- more browsers still search in parallel, but
    the actual outbound request rate to Google now stays capped
    regardless of how many are running. Retry passes tighten this floor
    a little further with each attempt, same reasoning as their
    existing item_delay backoff.
  - Also added explicit detection of Google's block/verification
    interstitial (title/body text, or a reCAPTCHA iframe) when a page
    load turns up zero candidates, so that case is now reported and
    categorized (in the run-log summary) as
    "blocked by Google (verification/CAPTCHA page)" instead of being
    silently lumped in with "no usable image on results page" -- which
    used to make a rate-limiting problem look like a content problem.
  - Note: the "try another candidate image on the same results page if
    the first one fails to download" behavior already existed before
    this change (see the per-candidate loop in
    _wie_search_and_download_one) -- the failures in the M3-128 run log
    were all zero-candidate pages (nothing to loop over), not
    first-candidate download failures.

New in M3-138 (IFM: "Load & Resume" button -- recover a run's progress
after the app itself never got to finish it, e.g. power loss):
  - Added a "\U0001F504 Load & Resume" button right after "\U0001F4C1 Load Folder" in the
    IFM image-pool row. Point it at the folder Automatic Search was
    downloading into (shown as "Downloaded-image temp folder:" near the
    top of that run's own .txt log file) and it reads the already-
    downloaded image files back, matching each one to a node in the
    CURRENTLY loaded .xmind file using BOTH the index and the node name
    encoded in its filename (see wie_download_and_convert's own naming)
    -- not the index alone, since that only still means the same node
    if the exact same file, sheets, and "Replace existing" setting are
    still in effect. A file whose embedded name no longer matches what
    that index currently is gets skipped rather than silently
    misapplied to the wrong node.
  - Matched images go straight into the image pool (so the on-screen
    count and Preview/Export already reflect them), and the mapping is
    remembered until the next "Automatic Search" click -- which will
    then skip every node that mapping already covers and only actually
    search whatever's left, instead of starting the whole run over from
    node 1 and re-downloading images that already exist on disk.

New in M3-137 (IFM Automatic Search: lower the browser count mid-run
without stopping, and a Resume button so Stop doesn't mean starting
over):
  - Added a live "Active browsers" control to the search popup (shown
    whenever more than one browser is in use). Lowering it and hitting
    Apply makes every browser above the new count finish whatever it's
    currently searching, then retire instead of picking up more work --
    no need to hit Stop and lose progress just to bring CPU/RAM down.
    This is one-way: it can only ever be lowered, never raised back up
    during the same run, since a retired worker has already closed its
    own browser and its thread has already exited -- there's no live
    thread left to hand more work back to. The reduction also carries
    forward into every later retry pass, not just whichever pass was
    running when it was applied.
  - Added a "Resume" button next to Stop/Close, enabled once a run ends
    (however it ends -- Stopped by the user, or naturally out of retry
    passes) with at least one node still unimaged. Resume starts a new
    search scoped to ONLY those still-missing nodes and merges its
    results back into the very same positional list the original run
    was building, so nodes that already succeeded are never re-searched
    or re-downloaded -- "Stop" no longer means "start over from node 1"
    the next time you want to keep going.

New in M3-136 (IFM: removed the M3-135 debug checkbox now that the wrap
fix is confirmed; hardened the label itself for 100k+ image counts):
  - Removed the "Test label with ~10k images (debug)" checkbox and its
    fake-number override -- it was only ever meant to eyeball the
    M3-135 wraplength fix without waiting through a real large run, not
    to stick around as a permanent control.
  - _update_structure_images_status now comma-formats every count in
    this label (f"{n:,}") so it stays readable at five- and six-digit
    (100k+) scale instead of turning into one long unbroken run of
    digits. Combined with M3-135's wraplength=250/anchor="w" on the
    label itself, this holds up cleanly all the way through 100k+
    images.

New in M3-135 (IFM: "X image(s) loaded this run (...)" label clipping on
both sides once counts got large; added a debug checkbox to test it):
  - Root cause: this label had no `wraplength` set, unlike the spinner
    status label right above it which already used wraplength=250. A Tk
    Label with no wraplength never wraps -- it just requests whatever
    width its text needs, and once the text got long enough (four-digit
    counts and up) it overflowed the panel's fixed width. Because
    Label's default anchor is center, the overflow got cut off evenly
    from BOTH sides rather than just trailing off on the right.
  - Fixed by matching the spinner label's own wraplength=250/
    justify=LEFT, plus anchor="w" so wrapped lines stay left-aligned.
  - Added a "Test label with ~10k images (debug)" checkbox right below
    it so this can be eyeballed at a large count on demand instead of
    needing an actual 10k-image run (which takes far too long) just to
    confirm the wrap holds up. Purely cosmetic: it only swaps in fake
    numbers for this one label's own text and never touches
    structure_images, the run's real loaded-count, batch sizing, or
    anything else.

New in M3-134 (IFM Automatic Search: "Retry passes" spinbox was being
silently overridden -- retries could stop before even attempt 1):
  - Root cause: on top of the per-node retry loop, M3-130 also added a
    mass-block heuristic -- if >=50% of a batch's still-missing nodes
    were categorized as "blocked by Google", the WHOLE retry loop
    aborted immediately, before making a single further request,
    regardless of how many passes the "Retry passes" spinbox asked for.
    A real run showed this firing right after the main pass itself
    (33/34 failures categorized as blocked), so the configured retry
    count -- 4, in that run -- never got a chance to do anything at
    all. The spinbox's value only mattered when a run *wasn't* getting
    hit hard by blocks, which is backwards: that's precisely when
    retries are needed most.
  - Fixed by removing the abort entirely. The "Retry passes" spinbox is
    now authoritative -- whatever number is set there is how many
    passes actually run, full stop, no matter how many of last pass's
    failures looked like Google blocks. The cooldown-before-retrying-
    blocked-nodes wait (also from M3-130) is kept as-is, since it only
    delays a pass, never skips one.

New in M3-133 (IFM Automatic Search: retry passes could silently never
run at all):
  - Root cause: the main search pass and the retry-pass loop shared one
    `try` block with only a `finally` after it, no `except` in between.
    Every per-node failure inside run_batch was already caught and
    routed through on_result -- but the call to run_batch itself had no
    guard. Anything raised above the per-item level (a worker/pool
    thread erroring before its own try/except even started, an
    unexpected failure inside _wie_run_auto_search_pool outside its
    per-item loop, etc.) propagated straight out of the main pass,
    skipping the entire `for attempt in range(...)` retry loop and
    landing directly in `finally` -- which still prints a normal-
    looking "Done -- X/Y downloaded" summary. Since this method runs on
    a daemon background thread with no exception handler, the traceback
    only ever reached stderr, invisible in a windowed/frozen build --
    so the run looked like it finished normally after only the main
    pass, with retries never firing and nothing visible explaining why.
  - Fixed by wrapping the main pass's run_batch call in its own
    try/except: any such failure is now logged loudly (on-screen,
    stdout, and the run-log file) as its own explicit reason, and
    `positional` is left exactly as-is (whatever succeeded keeps its
    image; everything else stays None) so the retry loop immediately
    following still runs against it instead of being skipped entirely.

New in M3-132 (IFM Automatic Search: Google only, no Bing fallback):
  - Removed the M3-130 Bing Images fallback below -- Google is the more
    accurate source, so a Google block/no-candidates result is now just
    a failed attempt for this pass, same as any other failure category,
    left to _run_ifm_auto_search's own retry passes rather than ever
    being answered with a different provider's results.

New in M3-130 (IFM Automatic Search: fall back to Bing Images when
Google blocks a query, instead of just failing it):
  - A real run confirmed the block from M3-129 is IP-level, not
    per-query: a fresh run minutes after a heavily-blocked one saw
    brand-new terms get blocked on their very first attempt, and the
    tool's own mass-block detector (M3-129) correctly stopped retrying
    early rather than grinding an already-flagged connection further.
    None of that can be fixed by retrying smarter -- an IP-level block
    needs either time or a different exit IP, neither of which this
    tool can manufacture on its own.
  - What it *can* do: not treat "Google won't serve this right now" as
    "no usable image exists anywhere". _wie_search_and_download_one now
    falls back to a Bing Images search for the same query, once, the
    moment Google's response is specifically identified as a block/
    verification page (_looks_like_google_block_page) -- reusing the
    exact same accumulator-script/candidate-filtering path, since it
    works off generic <img>/srcset patterns rather than anything
    Google-specific. This doesn't touch Google again (so it can't make
    Google's block worse) and doesn't need any extra setup from the
    user. The run-log now says which provider a saved image actually
    came from.
  - This does not help once *both* Google and Bing are blocking the
    same connection -- there's no code-side fix for that beyond time
    or routing traffic through a different IP. Proxy support (each
    browser launching through a user-supplied proxy/VPN endpoint
    instead of the machine's own IP) would be the way to do that in
    code, but isn't wired up yet -- it needs proxy credentials from
    the user to be useful at all, so it's left for whenever that's
    actually available rather than added as inert plumbing now.
  - REMOVED in M3-132 above -- kept here only as history for why the
    fallback existed in the first place.

New in M3-118 (IFM Automatic Search: no more hard-coded 8-browser
ceiling):
  - Removed the hard-coded cap of 8 on the "Parallel browsers" spinbox
    (both its arrow-key range and the `min(8, ...)` clamp applied when
    Automatic Search starts) -- there's no real reason to stop a user
    from launching more than 8 headless Chromium instances if their
    machine can take it, so it's now just clamped to a sane positive
    integer and, same as before, to however many node names actually
    need searching. Worth knowing: every parallel browser is a full,
    separate Chromium process, so pushing this very high will scale RAM
    and CPU use accordingly -- there's no longer a script-side limit
    protecting against that, so it's on the user to pick a number their
    machine can actually handle.

New in M3-115 (fix: terminal error spam + intermittent slowness from the
Search Images browser session):
  - Root cause #1 (the error log itself -- greenlet "cannot switch to a
    different thread (which happens to have exited)", "Task exception was
    never retrieved", and repeated "Page.goto: ... net::ERR_ABORTED"):
    the interactive, on-screen browser session ("Open Browser & Search
    There", headless=False) and the silent background fallback used to
    fetch a single image's bytes when a direct HTTP download is blocked
    (headless=True, used from inside the thumbnail-loading and
    Download-Selected thread pools -- so it can fire on a worker thread at
    any moment while the interactive session is still open) used to share
    ONE global Playwright/browser holder keyed only by whichever mode
    launched most recently. Whenever the two modes disagreed, the newer
    request would close() + stop() whatever was already running so it
    could switch modes -- which, when the interactive session was the one
    already running, rips the live browser (and its Playwright driver's
    own dispatcher thread) out from under the user mid-navigation. That's
    exactly what the terminal was showing: a goto() (or a queued
    tab-switch) still in flight on the interactive session's thread when
    a background thumbnail/download fetch on some other thread silently
    stole and killed the shared Playwright instance out from under it.
    Fixed by giving each mode its own independent holder AND its own
    on-disk Chromium profile directory (launch_persistent_context locks
    its user-data-dir exclusively, so two Chromium processes could never
    safely share the one directory both modes used before) -- they now
    run fully concurrently without either one ever closing the other's
    context. wie_close_persistent_context() takes an explicit headless
    argument now; the interactive worker's own cleanup passes
    headless=False so it only ever closes its own context, never
    reaching over and closing the fetch-fallback one mid-use.
  - Root cause #2 (the final "_tkinter.TclError: invalid command name
    ...!button" crash in the log, from _browser_session_worker trying to
    reset the "Open Browser & Search There" button's text): the browser
    worker thread's polling loop never checked whether the search dialog
    itself had already been closed, so it kept touching that dialog's
    widgets from a background thread even after the dialog (and every
    widget in it) had been destroyed. Fixed with a dedicated
    _dialog_closed Event (separate from the existing _stop_event, which
    is also reused by the unrelated Stop-button/download-batch flow and
    so can't double as "the dialog itself is gone"), set only from
    _on_close, checked by the worker loop to break out promptly, and
    checked by a new _ui_safe() helper that every worker-thread UI update
    (_log, status_var, mini_browser_btn, the extracted-images report
    handler) now goes through instead of touching those widgets directly
    -- it marshals the update onto the main loop via .after() and simply
    drops it once the dialog is gone, instead of risking a TclError (or,
    on other runs, the various inconsistent-but-not-always-visible
    failure modes of touching Tk from a non-main thread in general).
  - These collisions also explain the intermittent slowness: every
    collision under root cause #1 forced a full Chromium relaunch
    (cold-launch plus disk I/O against the profile dir) purely to fetch
    one image's bytes in the background, and a batch of concurrent
    thumbnail fetches (up to 8 at once) racing for the same lock could
    each retrigger it in turn -- a burst of several full browser
    relaunches back to back, serialized behind one lock, is exactly the
    kind of stall that would show up as "the file becomes very slow"
    with no obvious cause in the UI. With the two modes no longer able to
    evict each other, that relaunch storm no longer happens.

New in M3-114 (per-loaded-.xmind level counts, all 3 AMX sections):
  - Each of the three independent "Load .xmind File" areas -- the F-Node
    Section's AMX Section, IFM's own AMX Section, and RXF's Source .xmind
    box -- now shows the same lvl2=N/lvl3=N/... breakdown and a
    "Total Nodes: N" line that the manual hierarchy-text entry panel has
    always shown above its Copy/Paste/Clear/.../Manual button row, applied
    instead to whatever .xmind file is currently loaded in that section.
    It appears directly below that section's existing status label (the
    one that already shows the loaded filename), wraps onto extra rows the
    same adaptive way the manual-entry version does, and clears back to
    "Total Nodes: 0" whenever that section's file is cleared.
  - Counts start at level 2 (the root/level-1 node isn't broken out as its
    own row, same convention as the manual-entry version) but the root
    IS included in Total Nodes.
  - F-Node's AMX Section counts reflect whichever sheet is currently
    selected in its sheet dropdown, and refresh automatically when that
    selection changes. IFM's AMX Section always counts sheet 1, matching
    where IFM actually pastes its images. RXF's box sums counts across
    every sheet in the loaded file, since Level Design / Color Capacity
    below it apply to the whole document, not just one sheet.

New in M3-113 (fix: Search Images extractor missing most images on
virtualized/infinite-scroll grids -- Pinterest, etc.):
  - Root cause: the injected "Extract images" button only ever scanned
    whatever was in the live DOM at the instant it was clicked. Sites like
    Pinterest virtualize their grid -- as you scroll, pins that scroll out
    of view get their <img> nodes either removed from the DOM entirely or
    recycled in place (same node, its src/srcset swapped to the next pin)
    to keep memory/DOM size down. So even after manually scrolling past
    40+ pins, only the ~15-25 pins still mounted at click time were ever
    in reach of a single scan -- everything scrolled past earlier was
    already gone by the time "Extract images" ran.
  - Fixed by switching from "scan once on click" to continuous
    accumulation: a MutationObserver is installed (via the same
    page.add_init_script, so it's live from the very start of the page
    load) that watches the whole document for both added nodes
    (childList/subtree) and attribute changes on src/srcset/style/
    data-src/href (attributes/attributeFilter) -- the latter specifically
    to catch recycled-node virtualization, where a pin's <img> node never
    leaves the DOM but has its src swapped as it's reused for the next
    row scrolled into view. Every match found (by either path) is added
    to a persistent window.__wieAccumulatedUrls Set that survives later
    unmounts/recycles, instead of a fresh local Set rebuilt from a single
    querySelectorAll('*') pass on every click.
  - Clicking "Extract images" now reports the full accumulated set (plus
    one last full-document scan to catch anything currently mounted that
    mutations may have missed, e.g. images already present before the
    observer attached) rather than only what a single fresh scan sees --
    so the reported count now matches everything that has scrolled through
    the page in that session, including the 40+ Pinterest pins that used
    to get dropped.
  - The Set persists per page/document (i.e. resets on navigation, which
    is correct -- a fresh page is a fresh window/global scope) but not on
    scrolling within the same single-page-app view, which is exactly the
    Pinterest case.

New in M3-112 (webpage image search, integrated):
  - Folded in the standalone "Webpage Image Extractor (Browser-Only Edition)"
    tool as an in-app dialog (see ImageSearchDialog, just above
    MindMapExporterApp) instead of a separate script -- it can't run as its
    own tk.Tk() app anymore since only one Tk mainloop can exist per process;
    it's now a tk.Toplevel opened on top of this app's own root window.
  - Added a prominent, bordered "\U0001F50D Search Images" button just below
    the existing "\U0001F5BC Load Images" row in both the F-Node Section
    (F-Node Spacing / Size box) and the Image for mindmap (IFM) Section.
    Clicking it opens the search dialog: type keywords, optionally restrict
    to one site, click "Open Browser & Search There" to open a real visible
    Chromium window, browse/click the in-page "Extract images" button, then
    "Download Selected". The resulting file paths are appended straight into
    that section's own image list (self.floating_node_images or
    self.structure_images) -- functionally identical to picking the same
    files by hand with "Load Images".
  - Downloaded images land in a fresh directory under the OS temp folder
    (tempfile.mkdtemp, i.e. %TEMP% on Windows) rather than anywhere the user
    has to create or manage -- see ImageSearchDialog._make_out_dir. Each
    section (F-Node / IFM) tracks the temp folder(s) its own searches
    created; pressing that section's "✕" button deletes them immediately
    (see _cleanup_search_temp_dirs), and any left over are removed
    automatically when the app exits (see _wie_cleanup_temp_dirs). Images
    picked by hand with "Load Images" / "Load Folder" are the user's own
    files and are never touched by this cleanup.
  - The search dialog now opens maximized so the thumbnail preview grid has
    room to actually show what was extracted.

New in M3-86 (layout fix):
  - The lvl-count labels now genuinely start at bf_container's top-left
    corner. The frame holding them was only anchor="w" with its width
    force-set separately (read from bf, applied after the fact) -- close
    enough most of the time but able to lag or mismatch bf_container's
    real cavity width and show up misaligned. Switched the frame to
    fill=tk.X so it always spans bf_container's exact width, and the
    reflow now measures the frame's own width instead of bf's, so x=0
    inside it is unambiguously bf_container's true left edge -- same
    column as the Copy button underneath it.
  - Fixed the Export box (ef) visibly stretching taller the more lvl
    rows there were. Cause: ef was packed with fill=tk.Y, i.e. "stretch
    to match this row's height" -- and the row's height is set by its
    tallest child, which became bf_container once the lvl-label reflow
    (M3-88) started wrapping onto multiple rows for maps with many
    levels. So more levels -> taller bf_container -> taller bottom_bar
    row -> ef stretched to match, even though ef's own content never
    changed. bf was never *supposed* to influence ef; fill=Y just made
    ef mirror whatever height its tallest sibling happened to have.
    Fixed by dropping fill=Y and anchoring ef to the bottom of the row
    instead (anchor="s"), matching bf_container's own anchor="s" -- ef
    now keeps its natural size and stays bottom-flush with bf_container
    regardless of how many lvl rows there are.

New in M3-88 (layout fix):
  - The lvl-count labels (lvl1=N, lvl2=N, ...) moved from their own
    full-width row above bottom_bar into bf_container, stacked directly
    above the Copy/Paste/Clear/... button row -- filling the empty gap
    that appeared there once that button row got bottom-anchored (M3-87),
    instead of leaving it blank. They wrap/reflow against bf's own
    measured width specifically (not the full panel width), so this
    whole column -- lvl labels, buttons, and the Total Nodes label --
    stays confined to its own side=LEFT container next to ef and can
    never extend under or behind the Export box.

New in M3-87 (layout fix corrections):
  - The Export box (ef) is now packed side=RIGHT instead of being
    stretched with fill=BOTH/expand=True -- the stretching approach in
    M3-86 inflated ef's own internal padding rather than actually moving
    the box, which read as "added padding" rather than "moved right".
    side=RIGHT places it flush against the right edge of its row (which
    already spans the full panel width) without changing its own size at
    all -- closing the gap to the right panel by repositioning, not
    resizing.
  - Fixed bf_container's anchor="s" (bottom) having no visible effect: it
    was also packed with fill=Y, which stretches the container itself to
    fill the whole row's height -- leaving no leftover space within it
    for the anchor to position against, so its content (the button row +
    Total Nodes label) stayed pinned to the top regardless of the anchor
    setting. Removed fill=Y so the container keeps its natural (short)
    height and anchor="s" actually pins that whole group to the bottom
    of the row, level with the bottom of the taller Export box beside it.

New in M3-86 (layout fixes):
  - The Export box (ef) now stretches to fill the remaining horizontal
    space in its row instead of stopping at its own natural width, which
    used to leave a visible gap between its right edge and the right
    panel. (It was packed with fill=Y only, no fill=X/expand.)
  - The Copy/Paste/Clear/... button row (bf) and the Total Nodes label
    beneath it are now anchored to the bottom of their shared container
    (bf_container) instead of the top, so that pairing sits flush with
    the bottom of the taller Export box beside it rather than floating
    near the top with empty space below.
  - The Total Nodes label is now centered under the button row (bf)
    instead of left-anchored.

New in M3-84 (equation-rendering bug-fix pass #2, plus UI requests):
  - Found and fixed a foundational bug that was quietly corrupting most
    equations authored with the JSON-style double-backslash escaping these
    hierarchy text files consistently use (every "\\begin{...}" meaning a
    literal "\begin{...}"): normalize() only ever collapsed an *exact*
    2-backslash run down to one, so a matrix row separator arriving
    double-escaped (4 backslash characters, representing the intended
    2-character "\\") fell through to the generic cleanup pass and got
    wrongly reduced to a single backslash -- silently breaking multi-row
    matrices -- and a command like \tag/\label arriving the same way had
    its command-specific pattern match starting from the *second*
    backslash, stripping the command but leaving the first backslash
    behind as orphaned stray text. Fixed with an early, universal
    "collapse a doubled command backslash" pass plus a corrected
    row-separator regex that handles any even-length run, not just
    exactly 2.
  - Fixed \text{...} content being visible to the NEEDS_BACKSLASH pass,
    which had no idea it might be inside a text argument -- e.g. plain
    "sum" inside \overbrace{...}^{\text{sum}} was being mistaken for the
    command \sum and rewritten into "\mathrm{\sum}". \text{} content is
    now protected as fully literal from the very start of normalize().
  - Added \overbrace/\underbrace (with optional ^{}/_{} labels), \boxed,
    \underline, \cancel, \bcancel, and \cancelto -- mathtext has zero
    support for any of these (confirmed directly), so each gets a
    dedicated hand-drawn renderer (brace/box/strikethrough over the
    rendered expression) instead of leaking the literal command as text.
  - Added \choose (-> \binom), \multirow (-> just its content, dropping
    the row-span behavior), \sideset (-> its base operator, dropping the
    corner annotations), \genfrac (-> \frac, optionally wrapped in
    \left\right if delimiters were given), and \DeclareMathOperator
    (parsed and substituted throughout the rest of the same equation) --
    none of these are mathtext commands, and each needed real structural
    rewriting rather than a simple whitelist/alias entry.
  - Fixed \operatorname{...} being silently downgraded to plain \mathrm
    (losing its correct operator-spacing behavior) by a stale mapping
    that pre-dated confirming mathtext actually renders \operatorname
    natively; also used to fix \arccot/\arcsec/\arccsc (mathtext knows
    \arcsin/\arccos/\arctan as built-ins but not their siblings) by
    aliasing them to \operatorname{arccot} etc.
  - Fixed \mathring, \qquad, and \middle all being missing from the
    known-command whitelist despite mathtext rendering every one of them
    natively (confirmed directly) -- same class of bug as \overrightarrow
    in M3-83.
  - Fixed \surd (the bare radical sign) raising a hard parse error;
    swapped for the actual unicode radical glyph.
  - Added \vphantom/\hphantom/\phantom -- mathtext has no support for
    these at all; since they're meant to be invisible spacers anyway,
    dropping them entirely (command + argument) is correct, not just a
    fallback.
  - Equation Settings: the Color, BG, and Format override checkboxes now
    default to checked, per request (Width/Height/Font remain unchecked).
  - Normal-mode text area: each hierarchy line's structure is now
    highlighted live -- the leading address ("lvl1[1]lvl2[3]...") gets a
    black background, the node name light sapphire, and the note/
    equation/label field values light green/light purple/light yellow
    respectively. Runs on every edit (typing, pasting, or Manual mode
    syncing back), not just on load.

New in M3-83 (equation-rendering bug-fix pass):
  - Fixed \overrightarrow{} losing its backslash and rendering as plain text
    ("overrightarrow{AB}") -- an asymmetry where mathtext's own symbol table
    recognizes \overleftarrow but not \overrightarrow, which the normalizer's
    known-command whitelist didn't account for.
  - Fixed \overset{...}{...} and \underset{...}{...} silently discarding
    their first argument (e.g. \overset{\rightarrow}{x} used to collapse to
    plain "x", losing the arrow) -- mathtext actually renders both natively,
    unlike \stackrel, which is the only one of the three that still needs
    the lossy "keep the base, drop the annotation" fallback.
  - Added a dedicated hand-drawn renderer for \xrightarrow{...} /
    \xleftarrow{...} (with an optional [...] label underneath), since
    mathtext has no support for amsmath's extensible arrows at all.
  - Fixed Vmatrix (double vertical bars) and smallmatrix being missing from
    the matrix-grid environment list, which meant their entire contents got
    thrown away and replaced with a literal "\mathrm{Vmatrix}" /
    "\mathrm{smallmatrix}" placeholder instead of being drawn. Both are now
    drawn like every other matrix type, with a real double-bar delimiter for
    Vmatrix and compact \scriptstyle-like sizing for smallmatrix.
  - Fixed hand-drawn parentheses/braces becoming visually invisible on tall
    matrices (e.g. a matrix whose own cells are matrices) -- they used to be
    a single fixed-angle arc across the whole height, which flattens out to
    a near-straight line at extreme aspect ratios even though something was
    technically being drawn. Now driven by a curve parameterized by
    normalized height, so the curvature stays visible at any size.
  - Added real \hline / \cline{a-b} support in array environments -- these
    aren't mathtext commands, so they used to fall through to the generic
    cell renderer and show up as literal text ("hline", "cline12"). Now
    extracted before row-splitting and drawn as actual horizontal rules
    (full-width for \hline, column-range-limited for \cline).
  - Added \multicolumn{n}{align}{content} support -- likewise used to leak
    through as literal defanged text ("multicolumn2cHeader"); now unwrapped
    into a real spanning cell centered across the columns it covers.
  - Fixed leftover preview .xmind temp files accumulating in the temp
    folder: the existing _register_temp/_cleanup_all_temps atexit safety
    net was never actually wired up to the preview file it was meant to
    protect, so a preview created and then the app closed before its
    30-second delayed-delete timer fired was never cleaned up. Previews are
    now registered with that safety net, the delayed delete retries a few
    times (in case an external viewer still has the file open when the
    timer first fires), and a startup sweep clears out any stale preview
    files left behind by earlier runs.

New in M3-82:
  - Fixed: main search bar (Find All / Enter) actually searches now -- it was
    clearing the entry box before reading it, so it always searched for "".
  - Main search bar is only shown in Normal mode; it is hidden while the Eq
    Adjuster panel is open (same as it already was for Manual mode).
  - Eq Adjuster panel reworked: Focus mode removed (no longer needed). It now
    shows a collapsible, searchable list of every node that has an equation.
    Click any node in the list to instantly show its equation image.
    Type in the list's search box to filter the list live (substring match);
    the top match's equation is shown immediately as you type.

Previously in M3-81:
  - Search: Enter triggers search, Clear clears the search box.
  - Eq size adjuster: integrated panel showing ONE equation at a time (fast, no lag).
    Use Left/Right arrows to navigate, scroll to adjust.
"""

